#use(std/code)
#use(std/table)

#section{Syntax}::

#section{Text Mode}
	Plain text is content. Structure is defined by indentation (tabs). Lines
	at a given indent level belong to the enclosing block

	#table(header: 1)
		#row
			#cell: Form
			#cell: Meaning
		#row
			#cell: #code{#name(args)}
			#cell
				Call. #code{args} is host code (keyword args welcome; no commas — #code{,} is Racket unquote. #code{#use} alone
				tolerates them)
		#row
			#cell: #code|{#name(args){body}}|
			#cell
				Call with inline body. Body is markup by default; parsed to content and passed as the last argument. Braces must
				balance
		#row
			#cell: #code{#name(args): text}
			#cell: Call with body = rest of line
		#row
			#cell: #code{#name(args):} at end of line
			#cell
				Call with body = following indented block. The #code{:} is optional (see the row below); with an inline body a
				#code{:} and nothing indented below is literal text, not a body
		#row
			#cell: #code{#name(args)::}
			#cell: Call with body = rest of the #strong{enclosing block} (see Hanging blocks)
		#row
			#cell: #code|{#name(args){body}}| alone on a line
			#cell
				Body = the #code|{{body}}| plus the following indented block (the trailing #code{:} is optional sugar;
				#code|{#section{Title}}| and #code|{#section{Title}:}| are the same form)
		#row
			#cell: #code{#name(args)} alone on a line
			#cell: Sugar for #code{#name(args):} — body = following indented block
		#row
			#cell: #code{#(expr)}
			#cell
				Host escape. Statement position → statement (e.g. #code{#(define …)}); inline → expression, result converted
				to content
		#row
			#cell: #code{##}
			#cell: Literal #code{#}
		#row
			#cell: #code{# }, #code{#;} / #code|{#;{…}}|
			#cell: Line / block comment

	#code{(args)} and #code|{{body}}| are each optional. #code{#name} bare
	is a zero-arg call

#section{Host Mode}
	Inside #code{#(...)} and #code{(args)} you are in the host language
	#code|{#name{…}}| / #code|{#…{markup}}| re-enters text mode, producing a
	content value (Scribble semantics). Example:

	#code(lang: "plain")
		#(for ([x items])
			#p{#(item-name x) — #(item-price x)¥})

#section{Variables, Metadata}
	#code(lang: "plain")
		#(define price 42)
		The item costs #(price).

		#(meta 'date "2025-01-01")   ; writes document state (collect pass)
		Published: #(get-meta 'date) ; reads on resolve pass — order-independent

	Scoping is host scoping; defines inside a block are local to that block

#section{Blocks and Indentation}
	#code(lang: "plain")
		#list(style: "1")
			#item: First
			#item
				Second, multi-line
				#list(style: "a")
					#item: nested

	#list
		#item
			A block body is the following lines indented one level deeper. A call alone on its line takes it — whether the
			call is bare (#code{#foo}) or already carries an inline body (#code|{#section{Title}}|). A trailing #code{:} is
			optional sugar: #code{#foo}, #code{#foo:}, #code|{#section{Title}}|, and #code|{#section{Title}:}| each take the
			block the same way. (With an inline body, a #code{:} and #emph{nothing} indented below is literal text, not an
			empty body — so prose like #code{#code{index.yamd}:} stays intact.)
		#item
			Dedent closes the block. Nesting = indentation; no #code{"-.1"}-style encodings needed (a #code{list} function may
			still accept them as sugar — its business)
		#item: Indentation is tabs only, single tab per level

	#section{Hanging Blocks (::)}
		#code{::} folds the #emph{rest of the current block} into the call's
		body, without indentation:

		#code(lang: "plain")
			#section{Motivation}::

			Everything until the enclosing dedent/EOF is inside the section.
			#note: including this.

		#list
			#item: Pure reader-level transform; evaluation sees an ordinary body
			#item
				Recursive: a later #code{::} at the same level nests inside the earlier one. Flat LaTeX-style sectioning, if
				wanted, is implemented by the #code{section} function (level-aware), not by the reader
			#item: #code{::} at top level = rest of file (useful for #code{#template(...)::})

#section{Contexts}
	Host-language tweaks live in #strong{contexts} that are auto-scoped: by
	default all settings go to the current context. #code{#fork-context::}
	forks the current context for the rest of the enclosing block; block-bodied
	calls fork implicitly where it makes sense. Contexts also drive
	auto-detection, e.g. #code{section} stores the current header level in the
	context, so nesting depth determines #code{<h1>}/#code{<h2>}/… without
	explicit levels
